{:ev/id "lc-0053" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000052 :ev/type :concept-accepted :concept {:id "m-mcp" :title "loretile.mcp" :definition "An MCP server over one graph's log.edn, for coding agents (wiki/Profiles.md — the second consumer of a curated graph). Stdio transport, newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0, the handful of methods the protocol needs… (src/loretile/mcp.cljs)" :kind :module :source-id "src-mcp"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0054" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000053 :ev/type :object-added :object {:id "o-m-mcp" :concept-id "m-mcp" :kind :explanation :level :apprentice :locale "en" :origin :authored :text "An MCP server over one graph's log.edn, for coding agents (wiki/Profiles.md — the second consumer of a curated graph). Stdio transport, newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0, the handful of methods the protocol needs (initialize, tools/list, tools/call, ping) written by hand — no SDK, no new dependency. Every tool is a pure read from loretile.graph-tools; nothing here writes, so an agent can be handed a graph without being able to change it. node out/mcp.js /path/to/log.edn [--sources /folder/of/documents] claude mcp add loretile -- node /abs/out/mcp.js /abs/log.edn The file is re-read when it changes on disk (mtime), so an author exporting a new log.edn is seen by the next call. With --sources, each source of the graph is looked up in that folder by its title (then its ref) — text files only, never outside the folder — and two more tools open the document behind a node (read-source, search-sources): measured 2026-10-21, the raw document answered what the graph had lost." :grounded-in "src-mcp"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0055" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000054 :ev/type :source-added :source {:id "src-merge" :title "src/loretile/merge.cljs" :ref "src/loretile/merge.cljs"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0056" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000055 :ev/type :concept-accepted :concept {:id "m-merge" :title "loretile.merge" :definition "MERGE FILES (2026-10-31). Someone — a person, a workflow, a model, it does not matter — drops a log of events for a graph on S3 under merges/{graph-id}/. Loretile's part is mechanical: list the files, let the author… (src/loretile/merge.cljs)" :kind :module :source-id "src-merge"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0057" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000056 :ev/type :object-added :object {:id "o-m-merge" :concept-id "m-merge" :kind :explanation :level :apprentice :locale "en" :origin :authored :text "MERGE FILES (2026-10-31). Someone — a person, a workflow, a model, it does not matter — drops a log of events for a graph on S3 under merges/{graph-id}/. Loretile's part is mechanical: list the files, let the author pick one, and walk through its events as the SAME cards the intake chat deals, each change visible on the map before it is accepted. Picking a file registers it as a SOURCE in the graph's own log, so «already merged» travels with exports and needs no second bookkeeping store: pending = listed on the bucket − sources in the fold. Proposals in the file (concept-/edge-proposed) are appended on pick — the guard drops duplicates and re-proposals exactly as for the chat — so new topics and links are ghosts on the map at once. Everything else waits on its card and is appended VERBATIM on accept: the file's ids, actor and timestamps survive (provenance), and merging the same file twice is a no-op by :ev/id." :grounded-in "src-merge"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0058" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000057 :ev/type :source-added :source {:id "src-profile" :title "src/loretile/profile.cljc" :ref "src/loretile/profile.cljc"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0059" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000058 :ev/type :concept-accepted :concept {:id "m-profile" :title "loretile.profile" :definition "An ONTOLOGY PROFILE: the vocabulary a graph is written in (wiki/Profiles.md). A graph = a profile + its event log. `learning` is today's closed ontology, verbatim; any other profile is supplied by the author as an EDN… (src/loretile/profile.cljc)" :kind :module :source-id "src-profile"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0060" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000059 :ev/type :object-added :object {:id "o-m-profile" :concept-id "m-profile" :kind :explanation :level :apprentice :locale "en" :origin :authored :text "An ONTOLOGY PROFILE: the vocabulary a graph is written in (wiki/Profiles.md). A graph = a profile + its event log. `learning` is today's closed ontology, verbatim; any other profile is supplied by the author as an EDN file at graph creation and written into the log (`profile-set`), so it versions and exports with the graph. The learning engine reads its relations BY NAME (`:requires`, `:is-confused-with`, `:grounded-in`): those ids are reserved — a custom profile may not redefine them, so no custom relation can ever reach the scheduler, the closure or mastery." :grounded-in "src-profile"}}
