{:ev/id "lc-0062" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000061 :ev/type :concept-accepted :concept {:id "m-rdf" :title "loretile.rdf" :definition "The graph as RDF (Turtle), for an organisation's own knowledge-graph tools (2026-10-21, after Netflix's UDA: their domain models are RDF and SHACL). One way OUT — the boundary rule of wiki/Data-Model.md holds: a… (src/loretile/rdf.cljc)" :kind :module :source-id "src-rdf"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0063" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000062 :ev/type :object-added :object {:id "o-m-rdf" :concept-id "m-rdf" :kind :explanation :level :apprentice :locale "en" :origin :authored :text "The graph as RDF (Turtle), for an organisation's own knowledge-graph tools (2026-10-21, after Netflix's UDA: their domain models are RDF and SHACL). One way OUT — the boundary rule of wiki/Data-Model.md holds: a standard maps in or out, Loretile never adopts it as its format. What a reader gets: the ontology as OWL classes and object properties (labels, inverse labels, descriptions as rdfs:comment, endpoints as rdfs:domain / rdfs:range), every node typed by its class with its title, definition and material, every edge as a triple, a link the model inferred as a reified statement carrying `lt:inferred` and its reason, sources through prov:wasDerivedFrom, STALE and DEPRECATED flags, the competency questions, and who owns the graph. Pure, so it is unit-tested." :grounded-in "src-rdf"}}
