{:ev/id "lc-0083" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000082 :ev/type :concept-accepted :concept {:id "m-turtle" :title "loretile.turtle" :definition "Turtle (RDF 1.1) IN: a team that already keeps its ontology in Protégé, TopBraid or VocBench saves it as Turtle and starts a Loretile graph from it (2026-10-23 — the reverse of `loretile.rdf`). Two steps, both pure:… (src/loretile/turtle.cljc)" :kind :module :source-id "src-turtle"}}
{:ev/id "lc-0084" :ev/ns "loretile-code" :ev/actor "build.py" :ev/ts 1792000000083 :ev/type :object-added :object {:id "o-m-turtle" :concept-id "m-turtle" :kind :explanation :level :apprentice :locale "en" :origin :authored :text "Turtle (RDF 1.1) IN: a team that already keeps its ontology in Protégé, TopBraid or VocBench saves it as Turtle and starts a Loretile graph from it (2026-10-23 — the reverse of `loretile.rdf`). Two steps, both pure: `parse` reads Turtle into triples, `profile` maps OWL/RDFS onto a Loretile profile — classes become node types, object properties become relations — and says what it left out. The boundary rule of wiki/Data-Model.md holds: a standard maps in, Loretile never adopts it. The parser covers the Turtle grammar a modelling tool writes: @prefix / PREFIX, @base / BASE, IRIs, prefixed names, `a`, `;` and `,` lists, blank nodes ([] and _:x), collections ( ), literals in all four quote forms with language tags and datatypes, numbers and booleans, comments. No new dependency." :grounded-in "src-turtle"}}
